This is a demonstration of a proposed template for vertical timelines. It needs no JavaScript: it is wikitext plus TemplateStyles, and it follows the reader's light or dark theme. The sample content is only there to show the layout; check any dates before reusing them.

== Simple timeline ==
{{TimelineRail
|{{TimelineRail/item |date=1612 |title=The Go academy |text=The Tokugawa shogunate patronises the game and forms the houses of professional players.}}
{{TimelineRail/item |date=1846 |title=The Ear-Reddening Game |text=Shusaku's move 127 stuns Gennan Inseki.}}
{{TimelineRail/item |date=March 2016 |title=AlphaGo defeats Lee Sedol |text=AlphaGo wins four games to one in Seoul.}}
}}

== Several events on one dot ==
{{TimelineRail
|{{TimelineRail/item |date=2006 |events=
{{TimelineRail/event |title=Crazy Stone |text=Monte Carlo tree search is applied to Go.}}
{{TimelineRail/event |title=UCT |text=The UCT algorithm is published.}}
}}
}}

== Nested timelines ==
An item can hold its own timeline, indented under it, through the <code>sub</code> parameter.

{{TimelineRail
|{{TimelineRail/item |date=1960s–1990s |title=Hand-written programs |text=Engines encode Go knowledge by hand. |sub=
{{TimelineRail/item |date=1968 |title=First Go program |text=Albert Zobrist writes a program able to play a full game.}}
{{TimelineRail/item |date=1990s |title=Neural networks meet Go |text=Learning-based programs appear. |sub=
{{TimelineRail/item |date=1994 |title=NeuroGo}}
{{TimelineRail/item |date=1996 |title=Golem}}
}}
}}
{{TimelineRail/item |date=2016 |title=AlphaGo defeats Lee Sedol |text=AlphaGo wins four games to one in Seoul.}}
}}

== Collapsible items ==
Every item is collapsible by default: click a dot or date to hide the item, and its title follows the date after a pipe. This uses MediaWiki's built-in collapsible feature, so the template needs no script. Add <code>collapsed=yes</code> to start collapsed, <code>collapsible=no</code> to switch it off for an item, and <code>summary=</code> to say what shows after the pipe for an item with several events.

{{TimelineRail
|{{TimelineRail/item |id=ex1 |date=1612 |title=The Go academy |text=The Tokugawa shogunate patronises the game and forms the houses of professional players.}}
{{TimelineRail/item |id=ex2 |date=1846 |collapsed=yes |title=The Ear-Reddening Game |text=Shusaku's move 127 stuns Gennan Inseki.}}
{{TimelineRail/item |date=2006 |summary=Crazy Stone and UCT |events=
{{TimelineRail/event |title=Crazy Stone |text=Monte Carlo tree search arrives in Go.}}
{{TimelineRail/event |title=UCT |text=The UCT algorithm is published.}}
}}
{{TimelineRail/item |id=ex3 |date=March 2016 |title=AlphaGo defeats Lee Sedol |text=AlphaGo wins four games to one in Seoul.}}
}}

== Timeline sections ==
A red dot marks a section. Click it to collapse everything inside. Sections can contain sections, to any depth; each level is indented a little.

{{TimelineRail
|{{TimelineRail/section |title=Origins
|{{TimelineRail/item |id=ex4 |date=1612 |title=The Go academy |text=The Tokugawa shogunate patronises the game and forms the houses of professional players.}}
{{TimelineRail/section |title=Edo period
